  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing Metadata Service**\n\nI\'m currently observing the metadata service within the Google Cloud AI Studio Cloud Run development environment. It appears the compute engine\'s default service account is operational within the `ais-dev-...` project, with project number 4566928569. I\'m focusing on its behavior in this specific Cloud Run instance.\n\n\n**Analyzing Access Permissions**\n\nI\'ve hit a critical point: the server\'s default GCP service account, running in this container, lacks the necessary IAM Datastore User/Firestore Admin permissions on the external project, `cnc-copilot-abc26`. This directly causes permission-denied errors related to Firestore access, both for reading user data and writing it. Furthermore, the Google Sign-In failure is clearly linked to the Firebase permissions issue. The Firebase setup uses `applicationDefault()` credentials and the project ID from the environment, and the current permission configuration clearly restricts expected client actions, like writing.\n\n\n**Investigating Firestore Access**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the Firebase error, specifically within the `authMiddleware.ts` file. It appears the server, when attempting to interact with Firestore to manage user data, hits a permission issue. The `adminDb` object, used by the server, is receiving a `PERMISSION_DENIED` error when trying to read or write to the Firestore collection. This is happening because the server container\'s default GCP service account doesn\'t have the required cross-project IAM permissions on `cnc-copilot-abc26`. Interestingly, I\'ve realized the client, using the Firebase Client SDK, *can* directly read and write to the same `users/{uid}` path.\n\n\n**Resolving Authentication Issues**\n\nI\'ve determined the authentication issues stem from incorrect Firestore rules that blocked client document creation. I\'ve updated the Firestore rules to address this, enabling client-side user document creation. Furthermore, I\'ve realized the server can derive user roles directly from the JWT\'s `decoded.email`, eliminating the need for a Firestore read in many scenarios. This simplifies the server-side logic and improves efficiency. Now, I\'m focusing on securing the `GET /api/user/me` endpoint.\n\n\n**Refining Endpoint Security**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `GET /api/user/me` endpoint. I\'ve successfully pulled user information (UID, email, role, etc.) from the request object. I\'m leveraging the user\'s JWT to authenticate them. On the client side, I\'m verifying the existence of `users/{currentUser.uid}` within Firestore upon sign-in using the client SDK\'s `getDoc` and `setDoc` calls, and initializing the user doc if it doesn\'t already exist. My immediate goal is securing the endpoint, and ensuring the response includes the correct information.\n\n\n"
