  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing Identity Details**\n\nI\'ve noted the client email associated with the Cloud Run container: `ais-sandbox@ais-asia-east1-8ef17774abb64dc.iam.gserviceaccount.com`. It appears the Google AI Studio sandbox service account is the underlying identity running the container. I am making a mental note of this fact.\n\n\n**Analyzing Access Mechanisms**\n\nI\'ve determined that the AI Studio sandbox service account lacks Firestore permissions on the user\'s project, `cnc-copilot-abc26`. However, the user\'s Google account (`biju0716@gmail.com`) is authenticated with a Firebase ID token. This token, passed in `/api/*` requests as `Authorization: Bearer <idToken>`, grants full Firestore access to the user\'s project via the REST API. The browser\'s Firebase SDK leverages this token directly.\n\n\n**Addressing Authentication Issues**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the root cause of the error. It arose from the unexpected `FirebaseError: (auth/popup-closed-by-user)` when the user closed the authentication popup, which was misdiagnosed as an application crash. I\'ve now introduced handling for `auth/popup-closed-by-user` and `auth/cancelled-popup-request` to address this. While the authentication succeeds, the error is occurring later, in Firestore.\n\n\n**Revising Permission Strategy**\n\nI\'ve realized the `authMiddleware`\'s reliance on `adminDb` is a bottleneck. Instead of using it to check for user existence and get user info, I will directly populate `req.user` from the decoded JWT claims: `uid`, `email`, `role`, `displayName`, and `photoURL`. This sidesteps the need for Firestore permissions in the authentication middleware altogether, and should eliminate the permission-denied errors related to checking or writing the user document. This simplifies and streamlines the process.\n\n\n**Refining Role Determination**\n\nI\'m now implementing deterministic role assignment. I\'ve switched to determining the user\'s role (\"admin\" or \"user\") directly from the decoded JWT claims and a list of admin emails (`ADMIN_EMAILS`) on the server. I\'m also ensuring the user data includes `preferredLanguage` and `preferredController`, falling back to sensible defaults. The client-side will now be responsible for updating the user profile in Firestore if it doesn\'t already exist.\n\n\n\n**Evaluating Data Management**\n\nI\'m now focusing on data management aspects, specifically user preferences. I will now leverage decoded JWT claims to streamline data updates. I\'ve noted that client can update Firestore directly and the server through `/api/user/preferences`. I\'m revisiting `server/routes/user.ts` to examine the `GET /me` route; this already returns `req.user` which has `role` validated from `decoded.email`. Also, in `PATCH /api/user/preferences`, I will try to use `adminDb` and catch if unavailable. I\'m also now reviewing rate limiting and firestore rules.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_1111450"
      name: "view_file"
      arguments_json: "{\"AbsolutePath\":\"/firestore.rules\",\"toolAction\":\"Viewing firestore.rules\",\"toolSummary\":\"View firestore.rules\"}"
      thought_signature: ""
      original_name: "view_file"
    }
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 838090510
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
    provider_assigned_message_id: "L9TRaqifF9uc_uMPhaLroA0"
  }
