  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Calculator Code**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the `cutting-calculator/code.html` file. I\'m carefully examining the Stitch HTML, scrutinizing the layout, components, classes, and tokens used in the file. My goal is to perfectly replicate everything, adhering to `DESIGN.md`, while making sure to replace all hard-coded elements.\n\n\n**Refining the Cutting Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the cutting engine aspects, specifically `src/engine/cutting.ts` and `src/engine/materials.ts`. I\'m focusing on the input validation, particularly diameter, flutes, Vc, and feed, ensuring they are all greater than zero. I\'m also working through the formulas for RPM, torque, and chip thickness, readying myself to handle metric and imperial units. The milling calculations including chip thickness, power percentage and associated error handling is next.\n\n\n**Calculating Machining Parameters**\n\nI\'m now integrating turning, boring, drilling, and tapping calculations. For turning, I\'m including formulas for feed rate, material removal rate, chip thickness, power consumption, and surface roughness. I\'m also addressing warnings for boring bar overhang. For drilling, I\'m working with the formulas for feed rate, material removal rate, chip thickness, power consumption, thrust force, and warnings for depth-to-diameter ratio. I\'m adding tapping calculations using pitch and tap drill sizes, including the Fanuc rigid tapping block code and ensuring compatibility with cutting or forming taps. I\'m adding reference materials.\n\n\n**Defining Material Properties**\n\nI\'ve been compiling material-specific cutting data. I\'ve now defined parameters such as kc1, mc, and recommended cutting speeds (Vc) for carbide and HSS tools, along with feed per tooth (fz) for various materials including steel 1045, alloy steel 4140, stainless 304, titanium, grey cast iron, and brass.\n\nI\'m also working on routing the calculator. I am designing a responsive layout, with desktop having left inputs and right results, while mobile will feature a stacked input and results arrangement. I\'m focusing on user-friendly input elements, process tabs, material selection, numerical inputs with steppers, and sliders for cutting parameters. Live updating results and inline validation messages are in progress.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Machining Parameters**\n\nI\'m working on a 3D viewport now, visualizing a spinning cylinder as the tool. I\'m focusing on the display of crucial metric data in JetBrains Mono, 32px bold: spindle speed, feed rate, MRR, torque, cycle time, Ra for turning, and thrust for drilling. A horizontal power gauge showing kW vs machine max, with color-coded alerts, is also being added. I\'m creating a comprehensive warnings list and working on integrating an \"Ask Copilot why\" functionality using a specific POST request with defined parameters and model flash prompt for explaining the results to a machine operator, with risk assessment capabilities in various languages. I\'m also adding an activity row. I\'m examining the HTML for the top process tabs bar, inputs panel and overall layout.\n\n\n**Implementing the User Interface**\n\nI\'m now focused on the Imperial (IPM) input panel and the right results panel. I\'m adding a workpiece material selector that will display key material properties. The tool assembly selector will allow users to choose from Solid Carbide, HSS, etc. Stepper grids are added for tool geometry. Sliders and inputs are integrated for surface speed and feed per tooth. I\'m also adding Machine physical limits. The 3D view is being connected, along with calculations.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Metrics**\n\nI\'m now incorporating a core physics metrics grid, with spindle speed, feed rate, MRR, and spindle torque displayed prominently. I\'m focusing on the visual display in JetBrains Mono, along with associated notes like RPM, Vc, Dc, and axis limits. A cutting power consumption gauge is also underway, detailing kW versus machine max kW, using a color-coded horizontal progress meter for clear visualization of spindle load.\n\n\n**Constructing the AI Explanation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a systematic build: creating the `materials.ts` file, followed by the `cutting.ts` file for calculations. Then, I\'ll establish the `/api/explain` route and construct the 3D tool contact component. Next is the cutting calculator component, aiming for a precise match with the Stitch HTML, classes, and tokens. Finally, I will wire the calculator route and verify compilation.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_1715812"
      name: "write_to_file"
      arguments_json: "{\"CodeContent\":\"export interface ToolSpeedRange {\\n  min: number;\\n  max: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface MaterialSpec {\\n  id: string;\\n  name: string;\\n  isoGroup: string;\\n  isoClass: \'P\' | \'M\' | \'K\' | \'N\' | \'S\' | \'H\';\\n  hardness: string;\\n  yieldMpa: number;\\n  kc1: number; // N/mm^2 specific cutting force at h = 1mm\\n  mc: number;  // chip thickness exponent\\n  carbide: {\\n    vc: ToolSpeedRange;\\n    fz: { min: number; max: number };\\n  };\\n  hss: {\\n    vc: ToolSpeedRange;\\n    fz: { min: number; max: number };\\n  };\\n  note: string;\\n}\\n\\nexport const MATERIALS: Record<string, MaterialSpec> = {\\n  al6061: {\\n    id: \'al6061\',\\n    name: \'Aluminium 6061-T6\',\\n    isoGroup: \'ISO N: Non-Ferrous\',\\n    isoClass: \'N\',\\n    hardness: \'95 HB\',\\n    yieldMpa: 276,\\n    kc1: 700,\\n    mc: 0.25,\\n    carbide: {\\n      vc: { min: 300, max: 600 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.05, max: 0.20 },\\n    },\\n    hss: {\\n      vc: { min: 80, max: 120 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.05, max: 0.20 },\\n    },\\n    note: \'Free machining aerospace aluminium alloy. High thermal conductivity.\',\\n  },\\n  steel1045: {\\n    id: \'steel1045\',\\n    name: \'Medium Carbon Steel 1045\',\\n    isoGroup: \'ISO P: Carbon Steel\',\\n    isoClass: \'P\',\\n    hardness: \'180 HB\',\\n    yieldMpa: 310,\\n    kc1: 1700,\\n    mc: 0.25,\\n    carbide: {\\n      vc: { min: 180, max: 250 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.05, max: 0.15 },\\n    },\\n    hss: {\\n      vc: { min: 25, max: 35 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.05, max: 0.15 },\\n    },\\n    note: \'General engineering steel, predictable chip breaking behavior.\',\\n  },\\n  steel4140: {\\n    id: \'steel4140\',\\n    name: \'Alloy Steel 4140 (Cr-Mo)\',\\n    isoGroup: \'ISO P: Alloy Steel\',\\n    isoClass: \'P\',\\n    hardness: \'280 HB\',\\n    yieldMpa: 655,\\n    kc1: 1950,\\n    mc: 0.25,\\n    carbide: {\\n      vc: { min: 150, max: 220 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.05, max: 0.12 },\\n    },\\n    hss: {\\n      vc: { min: 18, max: 28 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.05, max: 0.12 },\\n    },\\n    note: \'Tough, pre-hardened chrome-moly steel. Requires rigid tooling setup.\',\\n  },\\n  ss304: {\\n    id: \'ss304\',\\n    name: \'Stainless Steel 304 (Austenitic)\',\\n    isoGroup: \'ISO M: Stainless Steel\',\\n    isoClass: \'M\',\\n    hardness: \'190 HB\',\\n    yieldMpa: 215,\\n    kc1: 2000,\\n    mc: 0.21,\\n    carbide: {\\n      vc: { min: 120, max: 180 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.04, max: 0.10 },\\n    },\\n    hss: {\\n      vc: { min: 12, max: 20 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.04, max: 0.10 },\\n    },\\n    note: \'Work-hardening tendency. Never dwell or rub; maintain positive feed.\',\\n  },\\n  ti6al4v: {\\n    id: \'ti6al4v\',\\n    name: \'Titanium Ti-6Al-4V (Grade 5)\',\\n    isoGroup: \'ISO S: Superalloys & Titanium\',\\n    isoClass: \'S\',\\n    hardness: \'36 HRC\',\\n    yieldMpa: 880,\\n    kc1: 1400,\\n    mc: 0.23,\\n    carbide: {\\n      vc: { min: 40, max: 70 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.03, max: 0.08 },\\n    },\\n    hss: {\\n      vc: { min: 6, max: 10 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.03, max: 0.08 },\\n    },\\n    note: \'Low thermal conductivity. Generates high heat at the cutting edge. Flood coolant mandatory.\',\\n  },\\n  castiron: {\\n    id: \'castiron\',\\n    name: \'Grey Cast Iron (GG25)\',\\n    isoGroup: \'ISO K: Cast Iron\',\\n    isoClass: \'K\',\\n    hardness: \'210 HB\',\\n    yieldMpa: 250,\\n    kc1: 1100,\\n    mc: 0.28,\\n    carbide: {\\n      vc: { min: 150, max: 250 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.08, max: 0.20 },\\n    },\\n    hss: {\\n      vc: { min: 20, max: 30 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.08, max: 0.20 },\\n    },\\n    note: \'Discontinuous abrasive chips. Can be cut dry or with compressed air.\',\\n  },\\n  brass: {\\n    id: \'brass\',\\n    name: \'Free Cutting Brass (C36000)\',\\n    isoGroup: \'ISO N: Non-Ferrous\',\\n    isoClass: \'N\',\\n    hardness: \'130 HB\',\\n    yieldMpa: 310,\\n    kc1: 700,\\n    mc: 0.25,\\n    carbide: {\\n      vc: { min: 200, max: 400 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.05, max: 0.20 },\\n    },\\n    hss: {\\n      vc: { min: 60, max: 90 },\\n      fz: { min: 0.05, max: 0.20 },\\n    },\\n    note: \'High machinability, low tool wear. Excellent surface finish.\',\\n  },\\n};\\n\",\"Description\":\"Create src/engine/materials.ts with 7 machining materials, Kienzle kc1, mc and cutting data\",\"Overwrite\":true,\"TargetFile\":\"/src/engine/materials.ts\",\"toolAction\":\"Creating materials.ts\",\"toolSummary\":\"Create machining materials database\"}"
