    thinking: "**Reviewing G-code Capabilities**\n\nI\'ve been examining the G-code parser within `src/engine/gcode.ts`. It\'s robust, handling G00, G01, G02, G03, along with arc centers specified as I, J, and R. I also verified the correct parsing of spindle commands (M03, M04, M05), feed rate (F), coordinates (X, Y, Z), and tool changes (Txx, M06).\n\n\n**Analyzing HTML Structure**\n\nI\'m now diving into the Stitch HTML structure for the G-code verifier, specifically from the user\'s prompt. My focus is on matching its layout, components, classes, and tokens exactly, adhering to `DESIGN.md`. I intend to replace hardcoded values with either real data or designed empty states, sticking to the previously established rules. I\'m starting by inspecting the exact classes and components, and noting the top navigation bar with the logo.\n\n\n**Defining UI Element Layout**\n\nI\'m presently focused on the high-density cards in the top metric banner. These are critical for user status and key performance indicators. I\'m nailing down the structure of each card, with a focus on \'Verification Status\' showing Safety index, \'Block Count\' that denotes motion, and \'Work Envelope\'. I\'m designing the live count of parsed lines and distinguishing types of moves. I\'m focusing on the component breakdown, adhering to styling rules.\n\n\n**Constructing Workspace Elements**\n\nI\'m now building out the fourth high-density card, the \'Feed & Spindle Check\'. It will have an eyebrow displaying \"SPINDLE & FEED RATES\", and show peak program values, such as \"S8500 RPM | F1200 mm/min.\" Additionally, I am including a chip that indicates \"G43 H01 MATCHED\" or \"CHIP LOAD: 0.08 mm/z.\" Finally, I am moving into the Workspace, creating the G-Code Program Editor with a header bar.\n\n\n**Designing Code Editor Features**\n\nI\'m currently crafting the modal state indicator bar that sits atop the code editor, featuring mini pill badges that show active modal codes like G90, G17, G21, G54, G43, and M03. I am building the code editor itself, and developing the syntax highlighting for line numbers, G-codes, M-codes, coordinates, and comments. Further, I am integrating inline issue markers that will visually flag critical errors and warnings, and am working on behavior that lets a user click to highlight or navigate to flagged issues.\n\n\n**Visualizing Toolpath Display**\n\nI am now focusing on the 3D toolpath visualization. I\'m building a Three.js (or high-precision Canvas/SVG) isometric renderer with a grid floor representing the G54 datum. I will represent rapid moves with amber dashed paths, and feed moves in glowing cyan. I will include a tool cursor, and render arcs smoothly. I\'m also including a collision warning beacon, with controls for stepping through code. I am focusing on making the controls intuitive.\n\n\n"
