    thinking: "**Analyzing Client Behavior**\n\nI\'ve confirmed a 200 response and HTML retrieval via `curl /agent`. My focus has shifted to the client-side execution, specifically `index.html` at `/agent`. The `createRoot` function within `src/main.tsx` renders the `<App />` component. I\'m now digging into `App.tsx` for further insights.\n\n\n**Addressing Agent Routing**\n\nI\'ve made a significant discovery. The `/agent` route doesn\'t directly display the agent view. The `AppContent` component\'s hardcoded `currentTab` and login-based conditional rendering in `App.tsx` controls the actual display. This means `/agent` either shows the landing page or dashboard, based on user authentication. Crucially, the absence of an `ErrorBoundary` anywhere in the app means any runtime error would result in a blank screen, a critical oversight!\n\n\n**Implementing Error Handling**\n\nMy focus has now shifted to implementing the `ErrorBoundary` component, a critical piece for robust error management. I\'m building a React class component (`ErrorBoundary.tsx`) to catch render and lifecycle errors. On error, it will render a red glass panel with the error message, component stack, and the source file extracted from the stack trace. I\'m also including action buttons for retry/reset and dashboard navigation to prevent users from getting stuck. In parallel, I\'m integrating a global error listener (`window.onerror`) for capturing non-React errors and directing them to a central handling mechanism.\n\n\n**Expanding Error Coverage**\n\nI\'m now expanding error handling beyond React components. I\'m focusing on capturing global errors and unhandled rejections using `window.onerror` and `window.addEventListener(\'unhandledrejection\')`. These will also render my red GlassPanel with error details, mirroring the React error handling. To ensure every route is protected, I will wrap each route within a `<ErrorBoundary>` component in `App.tsx`. This includes the `\'home\'`, `\'agent\'`, `\'calculator\'`, `\'gcode\'`, `\'academy\'`, and `\'kb\'` routes.\n\n\n**Synchronizing Route Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating URL synchronization into `/agent` and other routes within `App.tsx`. The aim is to initialize `currentTab` from `window.location.pathname` so the `/agent` route always correctly loads the agent tab, even with page reloads. I\'m also addressing browser back/forward navigation using the `popstate` event, ensuring the URL is kept in sync with the current tab. This is especially important for the `/agent` route itself.\nI\'m also working on dashboard component for undefined or missing data in chat lists or messages.\n\n\n**Refining Agent Component**\n\nI\'m now implementing the Agent component logic. I\'ve located the `<Agent>` component within `App.tsx`! I\'m now adding robust checks around all `chats`, `messages`, and `activities` usages to guard against null or undefined data, as the summary is undefined. This should prevent crashes and improve the user experience.\n\n\n**Investigating Component Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now integrating the chat component and associated features into the Agent component. I need to ensure the chat list and messages, if available, are displayed correctly. I\'m focusing on handling situations where the data might be undefined or empty upon initial load. I will check for these in `Dashboard.tsx`. I\'m going through the codebase for other locations or calls as well. My intention is to prevent crashes and ensure a user-friendly experience, irrespective of data availability.\n\n\n"
