    thinking: "**Analyzing the Error**\n\nI\'ve successfully compiled the applet. Now, I\'m carefully examining the precise error message returned by the prompt: \"[unhandledrejection caught]: {\\\"reason\\\":{},\\\"file\\\":\\\"Client Runtime (Vite)\\\"}.\" This \"unhandledrejection caught\" seems like a critical clue to diagnose the root cause of the problem.\n\n\n**Tracing the Source**\n\nI\'ve traced the origin of the \"[unhandledrejection caught]:\" string to `src/components/GlobalErrorHandler.tsx`. The code logs this to the console and uses it to construct a global error message. The question now is: what\'s the specific `reason` triggering this, and how can I replicate it? I\'m now looking at the possible `reason` types and extraction of the file name.\n\n\n**Investigating the Rejection**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial observation. The `console.error` triggered by the `unhandledrejection` event in `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` is actually causing AI Studio\'s test runner to flag it as a fatal error! Moreover, `setGlobalError(...)` is causing the entire app to render the `RedGlassPanel`, thus halting it from running. The `reason` logged was `{}`. I\'m now exploring the circumstances under which an unhandled rejection occurs during app startup. I\'m suspecting Vite HMR WebSocket closing or failing, but HMR is disabled, so there must be something else...\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Cause**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously examining the `main.tsx` file and realized that unhandled rejections are being treated as uncaught promise rejections in the browser runtime, if `event.preventDefault()` is not called! I\'m now exploring potential causes for the `event.reason`. Could it be related to Vite WebSocket errors or HMR issues? Specifically, I\'m considering the scenarios where the Vite client tries to connect to the Vite dev server\'s WebSocket `ws://...` within the AI Studio preview or iframe environments.\n\n\n"
