  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Connection Logic**\n\nI\'m currently revisiting the `testConnection()` function within `src/lib/firebase.ts`. Specifically, I\'m examining the connection test performed at boot, adhering to Firebase skill guidelines. I\'m focusing on the implementation details and how the database connection is verified.\n\n\n**Investigating Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously examining the error handling within the `testConnection()` function in `src/lib/firebase.ts`. My key concern revolves around how the code handles errors that *don\'t* indicate an offline client. Currently, the catch block only checks for the \"client is offline\" message, effectively swallowing other error types. I am concerned this may lead to internal promise rejections being unhandled. I have begun a search.\n\n\n**Exploring Rejection Causes**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `handleUnhandledRejection` function within `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx`. The recent `console.error` output, specifically `{ reason: {}, file: \"Client Runtime (Vite...\" }`, has piqued my interest. I need to understand how the `reason` variable is an empty object, especially given the various `reason` checks (Error, string, object). This leads me to believe the cause might come from the runtime.\n\n\n**Tracing Runtime Origin**\n\nI\'m now investigating the `\"Client Runtime (Vite)\"` file identification within the error stack. The `extractFileNameFromStack` function, particularly its handling of the `fileName` property and stack trace matching, is my current focus. I need to understand why the runtime context is being reported, and how this relates to the empty `{}` reason.\n\n\n**Uncovering Serialization Issue**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough. I\'ve realized the empty `{}` reason I\'ve been seeing in the console is a serialization issue! When an `Error` instance is passed as a property inside an object (`{ reason: error }`) to `console.error`, it gets stringified into `{}` because `Error`\'s critical properties (`message`, `stack`, `name`) are non-enumerable. This explains the mystery! Now, my focus is figuring out how to get the full error details through.\n\n\n**Connecting Error Source**\n\nI\'ve been re-examining the unhandled rejection handling, specifically focusing on how the error details are extracted and passed between `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` and `main.tsx`. It seems `main.tsx` attempts to extract the error message, file name (if available), and stack, however, `GlobalErrorHandler` is also logging errors, and passing the `reason` to `setGlobalError`. Now, I need to understand why the full details aren\'t consistently available in both contexts, since `main.tsx` attempts to parse the same data, but perhaps is handling it in a way that doesn\'t reach root.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_2512851"
      name: "run_command"
      arguments_json: "{\"CommandLine\":\"which google-chrome chromium chromium-browser node\",\"Cwd\":\"/app/applet\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":2000,\"toolAction\":\"Checking browser binaries\",\"toolSummary\":\"Check browser binaries\"}"
