  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering External Browser Usage**\n\nI\'ve learned the Chromium browser isn\'t installed in the container itself. However, the AI Studio preview environment cleverly employs an external, headless browser to inspect the app and catch those pesky console errors and unhandled rejections. It\'s using something similar to Puppeteer or Playwright.\n\n\n**Analyzing Error Triggers**\n\nI\'m now diving into the sequence of events. The automated error report was triggered immediately after a specific user prompt. The prompt requested explicit error handling for the `/agent` page, suggesting potential issues on that route. I\'m focusing on correlating the error report with the user\'s instructions to pinpoint the root cause of the `unhandledrejection` error.\n\n\n**Investigating Async Rejection Origins**\n\nI\'m digging deeper into the `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` file, specifically the `handleUnhandledRejection` function. I\'m scrutinizing how the code handles different rejection reasons, including errors, strings, and other objects. The console log gives some hints. I\'m focusing on why a generic error gets thrown when visiting `/agent` or `/`.\n\n\n**Exploring Vite and Firebase**\n\nI\'m now focusing on Vite\'s potential to trigger rejections. The WebSocket connection failure in Vite dev mode looks like a red herring; it usually just logs, not rejects. I\'m now shifting my attention to Firebase. I am revisiting the `testConnection()` function within `src/lib/firebase.ts`. It seems like Firebase initialization or a connection test could be the culprit. I\'m particularly interested in how the `try...catch` block in `testConnection()` handles errors, and if it propagates a rejection under certain conditions.\n\n\n"
