  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Addressing the Error**\n\nOkay, I\'m now focused on the specific error reported: an unhandled rejection within the client runtime. I\'m digging into the details of the rejection reason, and considering the implications for the application\'s overall stability. The \"Client Runtime (Vite)\" context narrows my focus considerably. I am considering potential causes and solutions.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Cause**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` file, specifically the `handleUnhandledRejection` function. The console output confirms this as the origin of the error reporting. The code logs the rejection reason and file. My next step is analyzing the different handling paths within this function. I\'ll focus on how it identifies the error and attempts to extract relevant details, especially the part dealing with error objects, strings, and other objects as rejection reasons.\n\n\n**Analyzing the `stringify`**\n\nI\'m now diving into the `handleUnhandledRejection`\'s error handling within `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx`, specifically the `JSON.stringify` logic. It\'s designed to capture the rejection reason, but the `try...catch` block suggests potential issues with how complex objects are serialized. I need to understand whether serialization failures are a common source of the reported errors, since this part looks like the best bet to find the problems, when looking at the last few steps.\n\n\n**Investigating the Rejection**\n\nI\'m now investigating *why* the unhandled rejection occurred. I\'ve considered that promises in the browser environment of a Vite app might be the root cause. I\'m reviewing `src/App.tsx`, specifically the `AppContent` function, and also the related `AuthContext.tsx`. I see an `onAuthStateChanged` function. I\'ve also found an interesting function in `src/lib/firebase.ts`, the `testConnection()` function, which I suspect might be the source.\n\n\n**Unraveling the Connection**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `testConnection()` function in `src/lib/firebase.ts`. It\'s clearly asynchronous, returning a Promise, and importantly, is called without a `.catch()`. This means any uncaught errors within it or its asynchronous operations will result in an unhandled rejection. I am considering `getDocFromServer()` or an unhandled exception thrown in the function itself. The catch block in `testConnection()` only handles a specific offline scenario. I need to figure out how to catch all of these errors.\n\n\n**Uncovering the Cause**\n\nI\'ve discovered the root cause! The unhandled rejection stems from `testConnection()` attempting to read a document in Firestore (`doc(db, \'test\', \'connection\')`) where security rules deny access. The catch block in `testConnection()` doesn\'t rethrow, which leads to this error. The permissions error from Firestore isn\'t caught, so it appears as an unhandled promise rejection. Now, to modify `testConnection()` to properly handle the permission error by logging it.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 475351550
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_CLIENT_STREAM_ERROR
    provider_assigned_message_id: "JvHRap_MIsi_qtsPhLCL2AI"
  }
