    thinking: "**Investigating Function Call**\n\nI\'ve discovered that `handleFirestoreError` is defined, but it\'s never actually called within the codebase. I\'m now examining `src/components/Dashboard.tsx` to understand the component\'s lifecycle and conditional rendering, focusing on the state when the user is initially not logged in versus when logged in. It seems critical to understand how the user\'s login state impacts the mount.\n\n\n**Analyzing Conditional Rendering**\n\nOkay, so I\'ve pinpointed the rendering logic when the user *isn\'t* logged in. The `App.tsx` file clearly shows that a `LandingPage` is rendered in the `home` tab. Similarly, for the `/agent` route. I\'ve also found an important piece of the puzzle in `AuthContext.tsx`. The `onAuthStateChanged` callback is *asynchronous*! I\'m now exploring how this impacts the overall flow. Specifically, `async (currentUser) => ...`, so `onAuthStateChanged` runs the passed async callback function.\n\n\n**Examining Login Flow**\n\nNow, I\'m diving deeper into `AuthContext.tsx`, particularly lines 44-61 and beyond. When a `currentUser` *is* logged in, the code fetches server profile information using `apiFetch(\'/api/user/me\')`. It\'s also initializing a user document in Firestore directly from the client SDK, using `getDoc` on `userDocRef`. I see a potential issue stemming from the async nature of these calls, and want to evaluate what happens if they fail.\n\n\n**Unraveling Cleanup Functions**\n\nI\'ve just realized a critical detail regarding cleanup in `AuthContext.tsx`. The `onAuthStateChanged` callback, being asynchronous, returns a Promise that resolves with the cleanup function from `onSnapshot`. This means that if `onSnapshot` throws an error, it is handled within its error callback, not directly by `onAuthStateChanged`. I\'m now exploring how this interacts with `Hero3DScene.tsx` in `LandingPage.tsx` to get the whole picture.\n\n\n**Evaluating WebGL Contexts**\n\nI\'m now investigating how the `<Canvas>` and `OrbitControls` components from `@react-three/fiber` create a WebGL context. My question is: do they handle context creation, and what are the implications of this in a headless Chrome/Puppeteer environment, such as the one used by AI Studio automated testing, which might not have a GPU? I need to know how to resolve the issue when there is no GPU, to enable the tests to run.\n\n\n"
