  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing Unexpected Behavior**\n\nI\'ve just noticed a curious behavior when stringifying an object containing an Error instance in a specific environment. The `reason` property, when stringified, yields an empty object instead of preserving its details like the message or name. Now, I\'m questioning how to access the error details within the object, considering the context of reason and file in JSON stringification.\n\n\n**Investigating Root Cause**\n\nI\'m now digging deeper into the origin of the unhandled rejection. I\'ve pinpointed that `GlobalErrorHandler` is logging the error, and the reason\'s message is being lost during stringification. My current focus is on understanding *why* the `unhandledrejection` was dispatched. I\'m exploring enhancements to `GlobalErrorHandler` to log the error message and stack. Furthermore, I\'ll review all async promises created during page load to track down the source.\n\n\n**Tracing Execution Flow**\n\nI\'m currently tracing the application\'s startup sequence to pinpoint where an issue might originate. The app begins in `src/main.tsx` by mounting the `<App />` component, which then renders a nested structure involving `<ErrorBoundary>`, `<GlobalErrorHandler>`, `<AuthProvider>`, and `<AppContent />`. I\'ve noted the `useAuth()` hook within `AppContent` relies on `onAuthStateChanged` within `AuthProvider`. The `firebase.ts` file seems key; particularly line 71, `testConnection()`, in `firebase.ts` is catching my attention.\n\n\n**Revisiting Connection Test**\n\nI\'m back to `testConnection` in `firebase.ts`! I\'ve realized its interaction with Firestore rules may be the key. Specifically, what happens when `getDocFromServer` encounters permission denied rules in `firestore.rules`? I see `getDocFromServer` calls a network service and rejects with \"Missing or insufficient permissions,\" which the `try...catch` in `testConnection` should be handling. But there is an `await` in `testConnection`, and I need to figure out how it behaves.\n\n\n**Analyzing Potential Causes**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the purpose and necessity of `testConnection()`. I\'m starting to question its initial addition, especially if it\'s being called on module load and consistently fails due to Firestore rules. The possibility of internal connection pool rejections adds another layer to investigate. My focus is now split between scrutinizing the need for `testConnection()` and considering alternative potential causes. I\'m also considering the user prompt from the earlier request, and considering the implications of that behavior.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Handling**\n\nI\'ve implemented a robust error handling strategy. I\'ve wrapped every route in a React `ErrorBoundary`, ensuring errors are displayed as a red `GlassPanel` with the error message, component stack, and file name. I\'ve also added `window.onerror` and `unhandledrejection` handlers to show this panel for global errors. Now I will focus on investigating the `agent` page imports.\n\n\n**Examining Error Source**\n\nI\'ve just revisited the error message and its origin. I\'ve confirmed `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` logged the `unhandledrejection`, specifically at line 59. The message format, `[unhandledrejection caught]: { reason, file }`, confirmed the test runner\'s error detection. Now, I must understand why this handler caught the unhandled rejection in the first place. My immediate aim is to decipher the event details.\n\n\n\n**Listing Possible Causes**\n\nI\'m now generating a comprehensive list of potential culprits behind the `unhandledrejection` events. I\'m focusing on scenarios specific to modern web apps and Vite, considering cases like a disconnected WebSocket in an iframe (Vite HMR). I\'m also including browser-related issues such as font loading errors, extension errors, and canvas/WebGL context failures.\n\n\n**Identifying Potential Origins**\n\nI\'m now investigating potential origins of the promise rejections in relation to Firebase Auth and indexedDB, particularly within iframes with partitioned storage. I\'m focusing on scenarios involving `signInWithPopup`, `onAuthStateChanged`, and interactions with `indexedDB` or `localStorage`. I\'m also examining the implications of dynamic imports, including failure possibilities.\n\n\n**Clarifying Unhandled Rejection**\n\nI\'m now investigating a discrepancy. I\'ve realized `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` also listens for `unhandledrejection` events, duplicating the listener. It logs to the console but doesn\'t call `mountEmergencyPanel`. This leads me to recall a previous user request. Now I\'m checking if `GlobalErrorHandler` is interfering with the custom error panel.\n\n\n\n**Examining Promise Rejection**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of the promise rejection, focusing on the `reason` object. I\'m aiming to identify why `handleUnhandledRejection` was called. To gain insights, I\'ll examine the properties of `reason`, including `message`, `name`, `stack`, `code`, and `type`. I\'ll analyze how `GlobalErrorHandler` processes this information. I\'m also scrutinizing the test runner\'s behavior related to `console.error`.\n\n\n**Verifying Error Stringification**\n\nI\'ve confirmed my suspicions regarding the `reason` object. When stringifying an `Error` instance directly, or nested within another object, I\'ve observed that all error properties are non-enumerable, leading to an empty object representation: `{}`. So `console.error` behaves as expected with this behavior. I\'m focusing on alternatives to access error data in the `GlobalErrorHandler`.\n\n\n**Analyzing Error Context**\n\nI\'m now investigating the specific error that\'s triggering the unhandled rejection. I\'ve realized `console.error` behaves as expected when logging the error, and `GlobalErrorHandler` is the cause of it. I\'m carefully examining EVERY promise in my codebase, particularly `testConnection()` within `firebase.ts` and line 71, considering a `Missing or insufficient permissions` FirebaseError as a prime suspect.\n\n\n"
