  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Error Origins**\n\nI\'ve been examining specific error messages in `bundledDevClient.mjs`, namely those related to WebSocket closures and transport disconnections, focusing on lines 711 and 590, respectively. Concurrently, I\'m noting that HMR is explicitly disabled, so I should be wary of any assumptions regarding Hot Module Replacement.\n\n\n**Addressing Error Context**\n\nI\'ve clarified the root cause: disabled HMR leads to expected, ignorable WebSocket errors from Vite\'s client, resulting in rejections. These rejections were captured by `GlobalErrorHandler`, but `handleUnhandledRejection` fails to filter them, treating them like a critical application failure and erroneously displaying the red panel, due to incorrect file context. I need to make a fix!\n\n\n**Investigating Handler Conflicts**\n\nI\'m now focusing on `main.tsx`. It also has an `unhandledrejection` listener! I need to determine if this listener is interfering with the existing error handling or exacerbating the problem. I\'ll need to carefully examine how it interacts with the `GlobalErrorHandler`. I\'m going to look at User Request 4 now.\n\n\n**Refining Error Filtering**\n\nI\'m now implementing more specific filtering within `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` to exclude harmless WebSocket and HMR-related errors, based on the identified criteria. I\'m focusing on ensuring these errors don\'t trigger the red error panel. I\'m adding checks for strings like \'WebSocket\', \'transport was disconnected\', \'ws://\', and \'HMR\' to the error string. This aims to prevent the error panel from erroneously showing up when it should not be.\n\n\n**Defining Error Conditions**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on more precise error exclusion criteria within `GlobalErrorHandler`. I\'m going to leverage `Str.includes(\'vite\')` or `file === \'Client Runtime (Vite)\'` alongside specific error message fragments from the `event.reason` to filter out harmless WebSocket errors. I\'m focusing on detecting strings like \"WebSocket\", \"transport was disconnected\", and \"the client is offline\", and also on \"ResizeObserver\" errors. I\'ve also realized that when the file is \'Client Runtime (Vite)\', and there is NO application error, `extractFileNameFromStack` can return this value; this is helpful. I\'m going to test the error handling with new Error(\"WebSocket closed without opened.\").\n\n\n**Confirming Error Location**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the error\'s precise origin. The `GlobalErrorHandler` is logging the unhandled rejection from Vite. The message is \"WebSocket closed without opened,\" which happens when HMR is disabled, and the `file` is identified as \"Client Runtime (Vite)\". The test runner simply stringified `{ reason, file }`. This is exactly what\'s happening. I need to make sure I implement a fix, and prevent these innocuous errors from being reported.\n\n\n**Eliminating Unnecessary Calls**\n\nI\'ve discovered an unnecessary network error triggered on every app start due to an unauthenticated query to `/test/connection` within `src/lib/firebase.ts`. This call, originating from the top-level execution of `testConnection()`, is blocked by `firestore.rules`. Eliminating this call will prevent these spurious errors, and should fix the unhandled rejection in the test runner. I\'ll remove the call to `testConnection()` and rerun the tests.\n\n\n**Implementing Error Filtering**\n\nI\'m now refining the error filtering within `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` based on the specified criteria. Specifically, I\'m integrating the `isIgnorableError` function to prevent harmless WebSocket, HMR, and other internal errors from triggering the global error state. This means WebSocket, transport disconnections, ResizeObserver, and related errors found within Vite/bundleddevclient stacks should now be handled silently, preventing the red panel display. This will involve checking the error message and stack for specific keywords and phrases. I will test these extensively.\n\n\n**Reviewing Error Display**\n\nI\'m now focusing on formatting unhandled rejections that represent genuine application bugs. The goal is to clearly display error messages, stack traces, and file names in `RedGlassPanel`. I need to prevent generic unhandled rejection strings from triggering the red panel. I\'m making sure that the error information, which should include the message, file name, and component stack, is accurately displayed, as requested in User Prompt 4. In `main.tsx`, the `mountEmergencyPanel` function will also be used on `window.addEventListener(\'unhandledrejection\')` and `window.addEventListener(\'error\')`.\n\n\n**Adjusting Error Handlers**\n\nI\'m now integrating `isIgnorableError` into `main.tsx`\'s pre-mount handlers for both \'error\' and \'unhandledrejection\' events. I\'m focusing on lines 34-45 to avoid early triggering of the emergency panel due to WebSocket or HMR errors that occur before React is fully mounted. This should resolve the issue of the panel appearing prematurely.\n\n\n"
