ction is verified.\\n\\n\\n**Investigating Error Handling**\\n\\nI\'ve been meticulously examining the error handling within the `testConnection()` function in `src/lib/firebase.ts`. My key concern revolves around how the code ha\\n<truncated 1689 bytes>\\nfocusing on how the error details are extracted and passed between `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` and `main.tsx`. It seems `main.tsx` attempts to extract the error message, file name (if available), and stack, however, `GlobalErrorHandler` is also logging errors, and passing the `reason` to `setGlobalError`. Now, I need to understand why the full details aren\'t consistently available in both contexts, since `main.tsx` attempts to parse the same data, but perhaps is handling it in a way that doesn\'t reach root.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"which google-chrome chromium chromium-browser node\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/app/applet\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"2000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Checking browser binaries\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Check browser binaries\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n{\"step_index\":403,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:40:36Z\",\"input_tokens\":4088,\"cache_read_tokens\":60649,\"output_tokens\":1291,\"thinking\":\"**Discovering External Browser Usage**\\n\\nI\'ve learned the Chromium browser isn\'t installed in the container itself. However, the AI Studio preview environment cleverly employs an external, headless browser to inspect the app and catch those pesky console errors and unhandled rejections. It\'s using something similar to Puppeteer or Playwright.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Error Triggers**\\n\\nI\'m now diving into the sequence of events. The automated error report was triggered immediately after a specific user prompt. The promp\\n<truncated 676 bytes>\\n Vite\'s potential to trigger rejections. The WebSocket connection failure in Vite dev mode looks like a red herring; it usually just logs, not rejects. I\'m now shifting my attention to Firebase. I am revisiting the `testConnection()` function within `src/lib/firebase.ts`. It seems like Firebase initialization or a connection test could be the culprit. I\'m particularly interested in how the `try...catch` block in `testConnection()` handles errors, and if it propagates a rejection under certain conditions.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"EndLine\":\"100\",\"StartLine\":\"1\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing firebase SKILL.md\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View firebase SKILL.md\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n{\"step_index\":405,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:40:54Z\",\"input_tokens\":61634,\"cache_read_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":1548,\"thinking\":\"**Addressing the Error**\\n\\nOkay, I\'m now focused on the specific error reported: an unhandled rejection within the client runtime. I\'m digging into the details of the rejection reason, and considering the implications for the application\'s overall stability. The \\\"Client Runtime (Vite)\\\" context narrows my focus considerably. I am considering potential causes and solutions.\\n\\n\\n**Pinpointing the Cause**\\n\\nI\'ve zeroed in on the `GlobalErrorHandler.tsx` file, specifically the `handleUnhandledRejection` function. Th\\n<truncated 1898 bytes>\\nthese errors.\\n\\n\\n**Uncovering the Cause**\\n\\nI\'ve discovered the root cause! The unhandled rejection stems from `testConnection()` attempting to read a document in Firestore (`doc(db, \'test\', \'connection\')`) where security rules deny access. The catch block in `testConnection()` doesn\'t rethrow, which leads to this error. The permissions error from Firestore isn\'t caught, so it appears as an unhandled promise rejection. Now, to modify `testConnection()` to properly handle the permission error by logging it.\\n\\n\\n\",\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n{\"step_index\":406,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:41:12Z\",\"input_tokens\":2098,\"cache_read_tokens\":66187,\"output_tokens\":109,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"EndLine\":\"200\",\"StartLine\":\"101\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing firebase SKILL.md lines 101-200\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View firebase SKILL.md lines 101-200\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":408,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:41:15Z\",\"input_tokens\":3734,\"cache_read_tokens\":66142,\"output_tokens\":119,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"grep -rn \\\\\\\"testConnection\\\\\\\" /skills/system_skills/firebase-integration-rpc/\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/app/applet\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"2000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching testConnection in skills\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search testConnection in skills\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":410,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:41:20Z\",\"input_tokens\":2351,\"cache_read_tokens\":67764,\"output_tokens\":96,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"manage_task\",\"args\":{\"Action\":\"\\\"status\\\"\",\"TaskId\":\"\\\"3768fe9c-394b-4869-a3b5-72b028948598/task-409\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Checking task 409\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Check task 409\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":413,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:41:23Z\",\"input_tokens\":2533,\"cache_read_tokens\":68000,\"output_tokens\":1331,\"thinking\":\"**Analyzing the Error**\\n\\nOkay, I\'m now diving into the root cause of the unhandled promise rejection. I\'m focusing on the specifics of error `0` within the 