 of tab styling, specifically how the `currentTab` state affec\\n<truncated 703 bytes>\\nt and messages. My new goal is to analyze the package dependencies of `/agent` and ensure they are appropriately declared.\\n\\n\\n**Confirming Endpoint Availability**\\n\\nI\'m now investigating the backend endpoints associated with the agent functionality. I\'ve re-examined the `server/routes/` directory and confirmed the absence of an `/api/agent` route. I\'m also confirming the absence of the corresponding `ShopfloorAgent.tsx` component in `src/components/`, which explains the placeholder content rendering issue.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/src/components/LandingPage.tsx\\\"\",\"EndLine\":\"140\",\"StartLine\":\"80\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing LandingPage.tsx lines 80-140\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View LandingPage.tsx\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n{\"step_index\":444,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:55:47Z\",\"input_tokens\":2659,\"cache_read_tokens\":87449,\"output_tokens\":242,\"thinking\":\"**Analyzing Navigation Code**\\n\\nI\'ve been examining the `Navigation.tsx` component, specifically the conditional rendering tied to the `user` state and tab selection. I\'m focusing on the button\'s `onClick` handler and the dynamic class names, aiming to fully grasp the tab switching logic. I need to understand how the `currentTab` state is managed and used to render the appropriate button style.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"grep -rn \\\\\\\"\'agent\'\\\\\\\" src/components/Dashboard.tsx\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/app/applet\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"2000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Checking agent navigation in Dashboard\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Check agent navigation\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":446,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:55:52Z\",\"input_tokens\":2607,\"cache_read_tokens\":88030,\"output_tokens\":1018,\"thinking\":\"**Analyzing Navigation Actions**\\n\\nI\'ve been examining the different navigation actions triggered from the dashboard. I see a pattern emerging with routes to the \'agent\' view, often including parameters like chat IDs and camera triggers. There\'s also some redundancy, as \'openCamera\' is repeated. This repetition needs to be understood.\\n\\n\\n**Investigating Parameter Passage**\\n\\nI\'m now digging into how parameters are passed to the \'agent\' route, especially from `App.tsx`. It looks like the `agent` route in `App.t\\n<truncated 594 bytes>\\nneed to update my expectations now!\\n\\n\\n\\n**Implementing Error Handling**\\n\\nI\'m now implementing robust error handling for the `/agent` route. I\'ve wrapped the route in a React ErrorBoundary to display detailed error information (message, stack, filename) in a red GlassPanel. I\'m also adding `window.onerror` and `unhandledrejection` handlers to catch global errors and display the same panel. This should offer a much better debugging experience than a blank page. Next, I\'ll audit imports for server-side code.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"find /tmp /root /home -maxdepth 3 2>/dev/null\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/app/applet\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"2000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching tmp root home\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search tmp root home\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n{\"step_index\":448,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:56:03Z\",\"input_tokens\":3338,\"cache_read_tokens\":88784,\"output_tokens\":87,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"manage_task\",\"args\":{\"Action\":\"\\\"status\\\"\",\"TaskId\":\"\\\"3768fe9c-394b-4869-a3b5-72b028948598/task-447\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Checking find task\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Check find task\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":450,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:56:04Z\",\"input_tokens\":2376,\"cache_read_tokens\":89961,\"output_tokens\":93,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"manage_task\",\"args\":{\"Action\":\"\\\"kill\\\"\",\"TaskId\":\"\\\"3768fe9c-394b-4869-a3b5-72b028948598/task-447\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Killing task 447\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Kill task 447\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":453,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:56:06Z\",\"input_tokens\":6764,\"cache_read_tokens\":92060,\"output_tokens\":5165,\"thinking\":\"**Observing Code Behavior**\\n\\nI\'m now investigating the `/agent` route and examining `App.tsx`, specifically the `getTabFromPath` function. I\'m focusing on how it determines the current tab based on the URL path. This function seems central to routing logic.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Navigation Logic**\\n\\nI\'ve shifted my attention to `Navigation.tsx`, and I\'m looking at lines 82-94, which deal with the \\\"Shopfloor Agent\\\" button. I am trying to determine how the current tab state interacts with the navigation components whe\\n<truncated 7224 bytes>\\nwill serve as the foundation for the `/agent` route. After this, I\'ll move on to the user-specified automated evaluations.\\n\\n\\n**Clarifying Page Expectations**\\n\\nI\'m now revising my focus. The user *is* seeing the placeholder, but they expect the full Agent functionality on the `/agent` route. I\'ll need to confirm what functionality is missing on the Shopfloor Agent page (chat, diagnostics, and more), and then ensure `ShopfloorAgent.tsx` renders this expected content, not just the placeholder. This is key.\\n\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"find / -name \\\\\\\"*Agent*\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/app/applet\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"2000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Searching for Agent files\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Search Agent files\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n{\"step_index\":455,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:56:41Z\",\"input_tokens\":5589,\"cache_read_tokens\":100324,\"output_tokens\":93,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"manage_task\",\"args\":{\"Action\":\"\\\"status\\\"\",\"TaskId\":\"\\\"3768fe9c-394b-4869-a3b5-72b028948598/task-454\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Checking task 454\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Check task 454\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":457,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:56:45Z\",\"input_tokens\":2410,\"cache_read_tokens\":103724,\"output_tokens\":93,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"manage_task\",\"args\":{\"Action\":\"\\\"kill\\\"\",\"TaskId\":\"\\\"3768fe9c-394b-4869-a3b5-72b028948598/task-454\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Killing task 454\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Kill task 454\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":460,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:56:47Z\",\"input_tokens\":3192,\"cache_read_tokens\":103905,\"output_tokens\":3904,\"thinking\":\"**Discovering Missing Files**\\n\\nI was thrown off course, as the Agent file was missing, which prompted an investigation into the project\'s foundational elements. I\'ve noted the successful creation of several critical UI components in Milestones 1 & 2: `CuttingCalculator.tsx`, `GCodeVerifier.tsx`, `KnowledgeBase.tsx`, `Dashboard.tsx`, and `LandingPage.tsx`. Now I\'ll proceed with a modified plan.\\n\\n\\n**Analyzing Placeholder Details**\\n\\nI\'ve discovered the `App.tsx` file contains a placeholder for the agent functi\\n<truncated 5556 bytes>\\n Agent. I\'ll integrate with the Firebase Firestore schema, specifically `/users/{uid}/chats/{chatId}` and `/users/{uid}/chats/{chatId}/messages/{msgId}`. Also, I\'ll allow a guest mode for unauthenticated users, ensuring `/agent` never renders blank. If the user isn\'t logged in, I\'ll provide local state or prompt them to sign in, but always render the UI! I also must handle `navigationState` and implement controller selection, text input, alarm prompts, camera/snapshot uploads, and voice input simulation.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/server/geminiClient.ts\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing geminiClient.ts\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View geminiClient.ts\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n{\"step_index\":462,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:57:19Z\",\"input_tokens\":5392,\"cache_read_tokens\":108177,\"output_tokens\":86,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/server/config.ts\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing server/config.ts\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View server/config.ts\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":464,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:57:51Z\",\"input_tokens\":2776,\"cache_read_tokens\":111336,\"output_tokens\":87,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/server/authMiddleware.ts\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing server/authMiddleware.ts\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View server/authMiddleware.ts\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":466,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:57:57Z\",\"input_tokens\":3732,\"cache_read_tokens\":111823,\"output_tokens\":226,\"thinking\":\"**Considering Authorization Flexibility**\\n\\nI\'m currently focused on how to make the `requireAuth` function more flexible. I\'m thinking about an optional authentication/demo mode for situations where a user is browsing or testing without being signed in. This way, we can support access even without the `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header, and I\'m figuring out how to do this gracefully.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/src/lib/firebase.ts\\\"\",\"EndLine\":\"60\",\"StartLine\":\"30\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing apiFetch in firebase.ts\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View firebase.ts\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":468,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:58:02Z\",\"input_tokens\":3029,\"cache_read_tokens\":113391,\"output_tokens\":80,\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/src/lib/firebase.ts\\\"\",\"EndLine\":\"95\",\"StartLine\":\"60\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing rest of firebase.ts\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View rest of firebase.ts\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":470,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:58:07Z\",\"input_tokens\":2904,\"cache_read_tokens\":114183,\"output_tokens\":219,\"thinking\":\"**Analyzing Authentication Flow**\\n\\nI\'ve been examining the `apiFetch` function and its integration with authentication. The key aspect I\'m focused on is how the authorization token is handled, specifically within the `apiFetch` function, as well as the authentication middleware, and how it rejects access when a token isn\'t provided. I\'m focusing on the conditions under which authorization fails.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/firestore.rules\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing firestore.rules\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View firestore.rules\\\"\"}}]}\n{\"step_index\":472,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:58:16Z\",\"input_tokens\":3378,\"cache_read_tokens\":114902,\"output_tokens\":1407,\"thinking\":\"**Analyzing Firestore Rules**\\n\\nI\'ve been examining the Firestore rules, specifically how they handle access control for chat data. The rules currently grant read and write permissions on `/users/{uid}/chats/{chatId}` to the user with the corresponding `uid`, and then further restrict access to the `/messages/{msgId}` documents within those chats to the chat owner as well. My focus is on the `isOwner(uid)` function.\\n\\n\\n**Addressing Client-Side Errors**\\n\\nI\'m now implementing the error boundary and global error\\n<truncated 1412 bytes>\\nuest, I want to present a functional UI for testing alarm queries. I\'m making sure that if the user isn\'t signed in, navigating to `/agent` doesn\'t result in a blank page. Also, I\'m carefully reviewing the imports, ensuring that the client-side code is clean, avoiding server-side modules by making calls to `/api/agent`. And of course I\'m working to handle the empty state gracefully, especially when a user first logs in and has no chat history. I\'m also planning to use `react-markdown` to format response.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"node -e \\\\\\\"import(\'react-markdown\').then(() => console.log(\'react-markdown OK\')).catch(e => console.error(e))\\\\\\\"\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/app/applet\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"2000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Testing react-markdown import\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Test react-markdown\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n{\"step_index\":474,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:58:28Z\",\"input_tokens\":3801,\"cache_read_tokens\":116500,\"output_tokens\":1175,\"thinking\":\"**Verifying API Endpoints**\\n\\nOkay, so I\'ve validated that `react-markdown` is functioning as expected. Now, I\'m shifting focus to the server. I\'m checking if the `/api/agent` endpoint, or a similar path like `/api/agent/chat` or `/api/diagnose`, exists. I\'m planning to create `server/routes/agent.ts` with a `POST /api/agent/chat` route. This route will handle incoming requests with a payload containing the message, any associated images, a controller, language settings, and the chat ID. It will leverage `ge\\n<truncated 1295 bytes>\\nor what types of input are available. Finally, all `.map` functions are guarded with checks for null/undefined values to prevent errors.\\n\\nI\'m now integrating features of the `ShopfloorAgent.tsx` component. This includes the controller selector, the camera/OCR image upload feature (with webcam and file input options), and the voice/audio input toggle. In the UI I am adding quick alarm presets to assist with common controller error codes. The component will also have interactive chat history functionality.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"view_file\",\"args\":{\"AbsolutePath\":\"\\\"/server.ts\\\"\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Viewing server.ts\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"View server.ts\\\"\"}}],\"truncated_fields\":[\"thinking\"]}\n{\"step_index\":476,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"PLANNER_RESPONSE\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-10-16T09:58:40Z\",\"input_tokens\":4297,\"cache_read_tokens\":118464,\"output_tokens\":568,\"thinking\":\"**Examining Authentication Logic**\\n\\nI\'m now diving into `server/routes/agent.ts` to understand the input requirements and capabilities defined there. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the authentication logic. It seems there\'s optional authentication via a bearer token. If present and valid, `adminAuth.verifyIdToken(idToken)` handles the verification; otherwise, the request is allowed.\\n\\n\\n**Verifying Gemini API Syntax**\\n\\nI\'m now checking the syntax for calling the Gemini model within the application. Specifically, I\'m examining the differences between single-prompt and multimodal requests, and how the `@google/genai` library structures these calls. I\'m cross-referencing examples from `server/routes/explain.ts` and the documentation to ensure the correct `contents` structure is being used for both text-only and image-inclusive prompts. My focus is on ensuring the data is correctly formatted for the multimodal requests, including base64 encoding and MIME types.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"nam