  planner_response {
    response: "To ensure the 3D simulation fills its container and provides maximum visibility for the operator, I have updated the layout in the Academy **Mission View**.\n\nI identified that the intermediate container was restricting the vertical growth of the simulation. I have now expanded the minimum height and ensured the `Viewport3D` component correctly inherits the full dimensions of its parent layout.\n\n### Changes Summary:\n1.  **Layout Expansion**: Increased the default size of the simulation area in `src/components/academy/MissionView.tsx` by setting a larger, more prominent height (`h-[500px]`) on the container.\n2.  **Component Robustness**: Verified that `src/components/ui/Viewport3D.tsx` uses full width and height utilities to propagate the parent\'s dimensions directly to the Three.js `Canvas`.\n\nThe 3D environment now occupies the maximum available space in the central column, making toolpaths and collision markers significantly easier to inspect during simulation runs.\n\n"
    thinking: "**Defining Canvas Dimensions**\n\nI\'m focusing on dynamically sizing the canvas within the `Viewport3D` component. My goal is to ensure it always fills its parent `div`, aligning perfectly with the Academy\'s structure. I\'m exploring how the CSS selector can achieve maximum size through adjustments.\n\n\n**Analyzing Viewport Structure**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the interaction between `Viewport3D` and its parent in `MissionView`. The issue appears to be how the `Canvas` within `Viewport3D` inherits dimensions. The `Viewport3D` component itself uses `w-full h-full min-h-[400px]`, but I\'m checking if the parent `div` in `MissionView` is interfering, especially with its `relative` positioning and transition properties.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Size Inheritance**\n\nI\'m now investigating height propagation within `MissionView`. It seems the parent `div` of `Viewport3D`, with classes like `relative rounded-xl overflow-hidden`, might not be properly defining its height, hindering the `h-full` inheritance by the `Canvas`. R3F\'s default behavior, making the `Canvas` take up the parent\'s full dimensions, is key here. The problem may lie in ensuring this parent div has a definitive height, so that `h-full` works.\n\n\n**Examining Layout Constraints**\n\nI\'m now revisiting the layout in `MissionView.tsx`. My current thought is that the user desires an enlarged `Viewport3D` container. The `Canvas` is already in a `w-full h-full` div, so I\'m investigating the surrounding structure and checking the `div` with `relative rounded-xl overflow-hidden`, which is a key component to the canvas\'s size.\n\n\n**Tracing Canvas Element Hierarchy**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the DOM structure to trace the `canvas` element\'s lineage. I\'m carefully mapping the path from `#root` to the `canvas`, identifying each `div` along the way, including the R3F-generated containers. I\'m focusing on the `style` props and inheritance issues that could be preventing the `canvas` from filling its parent, given it is inside a `w-full h-full` div.\n\n\n**Adjusting Minimum Heights**\n\nI\'m now considering the implications of `min-h-[400px]` within `Viewport3D`. Since the parent div in `MissionView` lacks a defined height, `h-full` might be ineffective. I\'m exploring increasing `min-h` or adjusting vertical space allocation to achieve maximum size. The key insight is that R3F\'s `Canvas` takes 100% of its parent, so the problem lies in the parent\'s sizing. I also need to make the container in `MissionView.tsx` bigger.\n\n\n**Optimizing Flexbox Layout**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how `flex-1` might resolve the height issue in `MissionView.tsx`. Specifically, I\'m integrating this class into the `div` containing `Viewport3D`, given the parent column uses `flex flex-col`. I\'m going to change the code to use `flex-1`, as it should cause the `Viewport3D` to fill available space.\n\n\n**Refining Canvas Sizing**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `Canvas` element. I\'m checking if explicitly setting `width: \'100%\'` and `height: \'100%\'` as inline styles helps, though it may be redundant. The path to the `canvas` through the DOM, specifically `div:nth-of-type(1) > div:nth-of-type(1) > canvas:nth-of-type(1)`, is what I\'m reviewing to ensure there are no unintended style overrides preventing the intended sizing behavior.\n\n\n**Revisiting Viewport Container**\n\nI\'m now re-evaluating the parent container of `Viewport3D` within `MissionView.tsx`, after taking another look at the DOM trace. I think there might be a problem with the height propagation from the root `div` to the `canvas` via the `div` that `Viewport3D` controls. Specifically, I\'m checking if the `h-full` on the `Viewport3D` component is being correctly applied to the R3F containers, and eventually, the canvas element. I am thinking about how to ensure the canvas fills the space the user wants. I need to make sure the root div is using the `h-full`.\n\n\n**Confirming Element Styles**\n\nMy focus is now on confirming that the parent `div` does not override the inherent sizing of the canvas. Specifically, I need to verify that parent divs don\'t have conflicting styles. I think that the parent\'s `h-full` is the main factor. I will confirm `w-full h-full` and inheritance are working correctly. I need to make sure the root div is using the `h-full`.\n\n\n"
